#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050467 :topic/difficulty 0.9028537013288132 :topic/id #uuid "4bf7862e-8b34-577f-9a7c-1f3cc4989b50" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096647 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096444 :question/id #uuid "e47a8da7-9276-5f4c-8ee6-1fd737c2e9ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-4277" :question/problem "Consider the diagram shown below, where $\\overrightarrow{PQ}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overrightarrow{PT}=\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\overrightarrow{QS}=\\mathbf{c}.$ Find $\\overrightarrow{TS}$ in terms of $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\n![](images/04/04ce04bf7c7f88d6578c74db0d7398a6d299d11bd9f60f4fc4b6e1bfb0129f3b.png)" :question/worked-solution "We want to find a \"path\" from $T$ to $S$ consisting only of the vectors $\\mathbf a, \\mathbf b,$ or $\\mathbf c.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{\\overrightarrow{TS}}} = {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{TP}}} + {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{PQ}}} + {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{QS}}}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/53/53940607b5cbf0220008e6e31ec60f2fd7fd2e4f70c5a8e95e7fcfd64064b095.png)\n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\overrightarrow{TP}} = -\\mathbf{b}, \\qquad {\\color{blue}\\overrightarrow{PQ}} = \\mathbf{a}, \\qquad {\\color{blue}\\overrightarrow{QS}} = \\mathbf{c}.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/81/810cce10eb330b1767a101d9107a9534c437daa123f13056a486a9fc96dbab89.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TS} & =\\overset{\\to }{TP}+\\overset{\\to }{PQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QS} \\\\  & =-b+a+c \\\\  & =a-b+c.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f0ec6b-9cb4-5246-9ac6-23b35e3b224e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector as a Sum or Difference of More Than Two Known Vectors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096691 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096486 :question/id #uuid "0b0dbbc1-d39d-5aff-9f16-075779d36659" :question/math-academy-id "e-5015" :question/problem "![](images/24/2420154d44b786c6aad54212760e9a37ce67dbdce94a7043039f0e824c5ef9bc.png)\n\nIn parallelogram $ABCD$ shown above, which of the following equalities are true?\n\nI. $\\overrightarrow{CB}+\\overrightarrow{BA}=\\overrightarrow{CA}$\n\nII. $\\overrightarrow{CB}-\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\overrightarrow{AC}$\n\nIII. $\\overrightarrow{DC}=\\overrightarrow{BA}$" :question/worked-solution "- Equality I is true. Indeed, $\\overrightarrow{CB}+\\overrightarrow{BA}=\\overrightarrow{CA}.$\n\n- Equality II is false. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CB}-\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{CB}+\\overset{\\to }{BA} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{CA} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{AC}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nsince $\\overrightarrow{CA}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ have opposite directions.\n\n- Equality III is false. Indeed, $\\overrightarrow{DC}\\neq\\overrightarrow{BA}$ since they have opposite directions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c015dcaa-2155-5d80-985b-21924de92b28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Equalities"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096794 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096603 :question/id #uuid "c7526e18-37a9-572a-a6a3-0384bbccf41d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9934" :question/problem "![](images/74/7431bd3281e4e49f4caaa5b701885fb64c58196b2256af9d8744decd9a0d0793.png)\n\n  \n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overrightarrow{PS}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overrightarrow{QS}=\\mathbf{b},$ $\\overrightarrow{PT}=\\mathbf{c},$ and $\\overrightarrow{TS}=\\mathbf{d}.$ Find $\\overrightarrow{TQ}$ in terms of $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c},$ and $\\mathbf{d}.$" :question/worked-solution "We want to find a \"path\" from $T$ to $Q$ consisting only of the vectors $\\mathbf a, \\mathbf b, \\mathbf c,$ and $\\mathbf d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition, \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{\\overrightarrow{TQ}}} = {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{TS}}} + {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{SQ}}},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/49/4902397acd28e2e5f85063ba55c576d3768360225a705f31a66bd460405dd5a6.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\overrightarrow{TS}} = \\mathbf{d}, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{SQ}}} = -\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/08/08086f7e09568f908aa4919a1156d52e465216221be8908e72a1fd90ec8843dd.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{TS}+\\overset{\\to }{SQ} \\\\  & =d+(-b) \\\\  & =d-b.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c43200-991b-5854-be1d-fa9db80f3725" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector as a Sum or Difference of Two Known Vectors"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1102 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048780}] :topic/title "Problem Solving Using Vector Diagrams"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051014 :topic/id #uuid "79fb6a4f-4564-5155-81f6-db81bd65e488" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096650 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096449 :question/id #uuid "2ff4e8a9-d5ef-5e00-bba9-b51e18bf1f24" :question/math-academy-id "e-4284" :question/problem "Given the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ shown below, draw the linear combination $2\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}$.\n\n![](images/32/325e4a450d89ee1f5db4afd237dbc7d58efafec72d080386788f0491cc35950b.png)" :question/worked-solution "- The vector $2\\mathbf{a}$ has the same direction as $\\mathbf{a}$ but twice the magnitude.\n- Likewise, the vector $3\\mathbf{b}$ has the same direction as $\\mathbf{b}$ but three times the magnitude.\n\n![](images/06/066784522a39671fe57b157c6c692f5c56295126b8ec6f150d917e783ab63400.png)\n\nNow, we can draw $3\\mathbf{b}$ starting from the head of $2\\mathbf{a}$. The resultant\n$2\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}$\ngoes from the tail of $2\\mathbf{a}$ to the head of $3\\mathbf{b}$.\n\n![](images/d1/d1dd084ad4f8f717afac515471bcba679fbe545f266435a57fb1c94d0d5c9cca.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8b30414-400e-5c93-98d4-82299b4eb570" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Linear Combination of Two Vectors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096651 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096450 :question/id #uuid "f8325830-9c74-5bf2-866c-1c31acdcc37c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4285" :question/problem "Suppose we are given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ where $\\mathbf{a} \\nparallel \\mathbf{b}$. Find $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, if\n\n$\\mathbf{a}+7\\mathbf{b}=(2\\alpha+\\beta)\\mathbf{a}-\\beta\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{a} \\nparallel \\mathbf{b}$, the linear combination can only be written in one way. So, the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a}$ must be equal, and the coefficients of $\\mathbf{b}$ must be equal.\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}1 = 2 \\alpha  + \\beta  \\\\ 7 = - \\beta \\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nβ &= -7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the first equation, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 2α + β \\\\\n1 &= 2α + (-7) \\\\\n8 &= 2α \\\\\nα &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that $\\alpha = 4$ and $\\beta = -7$."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2121109d-7b73-5609-a9df-1eeed257ab1b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Statement With Linear Combinations Whose Coefficients Are Linear Functions"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1105 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048816}] :topic/title "Linear Combinations of Vectors and Their Properties"}]}
